<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad al-Badr</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1926–1996</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad al-Badr was the last Zaydi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> and king of the Mutawakkilite Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>. He succeeded his father Ahmad in September 1962 but was overthrown a week later by army officers under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdullah-al-sallal">Abdullah al-Sallal</a>, who proclaimed the Yemen Arab Republic. He led royalist forces backed by Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> in the civil war that followed and died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1996.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-badr.jpg" alt="Muhammad al-Badr" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muhammad_al-Badr.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
